        <div class="prose">
          <h2>A road we know well close to Aberdeen</h2>
          <p>The A96 is a long route — it eventually reaches Inverness, over sixty miles from Aberdeen — but our coverage is based in Aberdeen and Aberdeenshire, so we're honest about where we're strongest. Breakdowns near the city, past Dyce and Aberdeen Airport, and on towards Inverurie are within easy reach and normally our quickest jobs. Further out towards Huntly, Keith or Elgin, we can still often help, but call and give us your exact location so we can tell you plainly whether we're the right recovery option or whether you'd be better served locally.</p>

          <h2>The Dyce and Aberdeen Airport stretch</h2>
          <p>This section carries a busy mix of commuter, freight and airport-related traffic, and roadworks or lane restrictions aren't unusual here. If you break down close to the airport, junctions can be confusing at speed, so pull off as early and as fully as you safely can rather than trying to reach a specific roundabout. When you call, mention the airport, a nearby roundabout or business park, or the Dyce area by name — it helps us find you quickly among several similar-looking access roads.</p>

          <h2>Towards Inverurie — where the road narrows</h2>
          <p>Beyond the immediate Aberdeen approach, the A96 becomes a mix of dual and single-carriageway sections as it heads towards Inverurie. Traffic speeds stay high even where the road narrows to one lane each way, and overtaking manoeuvres on the single-carriageway stretches mean vehicles can be travelling close to the centre line. If you break down here, don't assume there's more room than there is.</p>

          <h2>Driving — and breaking down — on a rural single carriageway</h2>
          <p>Much of the A96 outside the immediate city area is single carriageway, and that calls for different thinking to a motorway-style breakdown. There's no central reservation, so get the vehicle fully onto the verge rather than just the edge of the lane, and check the ground will actually hold the car's weight before you commit — soft verges after rain are common in Aberdeenshire. Make yourself visible: a high-vis vest, if you have one, and hazard lights are far more important here than on a well-lit urban road, particularly around dusk or on an overcast day when passing traffic sees you later. Watch and listen for farm vehicles and livestock movements, which use this road more than most of the A90, and give them room. If there's no safe verge at all, it's often better to keep going gently to the next passing place or field entrance than to stop in a genuinely blind spot.</p>

          <div class="callout">
            <p><strong>Rural stretches after dark.</strong> Away from Aberdeen and Dyce, sections of the A96 have no street lighting. If you break down at night, put your hazards on straight away, use a torch or phone light to make yourself visible while you move to a safe position off the road, and stay well back from the carriageway edge until we arrive.</p>
          </div>

          <h2>Something other than a full recovery?</h2>
          <p>If your problem might be fixable at the roadside — a flat battery or a puncture, for example — our <a href="../services/roadside-assistance-aberdeen.html">roadside assistance</a> service may get you moving again without a full tow. Tell us what's happening and we'll advise on the best option.</p>
        </div>

