        <div class="faq-list">
          <details class="faq-item">
            <summary>Do you cover the whole A96, including near Elgin and Inverness?<span class="icon" aria-hidden="true"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><line x1="12" y1="5" x2="12" y2="19"/><line x1="5" y1="12" x2="19" y2="12"/></svg></span></summary>
            <p>Our coverage is Aberdeen and Aberdeenshire, so we're strongest on the A96 near Aberdeen, Dyce and Inverurie. If you've broken down further up the route towards Huntly, Keith, Elgin or Inverness, call us anyway and tell us your exact location — we'll be upfront about whether we can reach you or whether a more local recovery operator would get to you faster.</p>
          </details>
          <details class="faq-item">
            <summary>What should I do if I break down on the A96 near Aberdeen Airport?<span class="icon" aria-hidden="true"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><line x1="12" y1="5" x2="12" y2="19"/><line x1="5" y1="12" x2="19" y2="12"/></svg></span></summary>
            <p>The stretch near Dyce and the airport carries a mix of local, airport and through traffic, often with roadworks or diversions. Get as far onto the verge as you can, keep hazards on, and mention the airport, a nearby roundabout, or the Dyce area specifically when you call so we can find you among the various access roads.</p>
          </details>
          <details class="faq-item">
            <summary>The road is single carriageway where I've stopped — is that more dangerous than a dual carriageway breakdown?<span class="icon" aria-hidden="true"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><line x1="12" y1="5" x2="12" y2="19"/><line x1="5" y1="12" x2="19" y2="12"/></svg></span></summary>
            <p>It brings different risks rather than automatically more of them. There's no central reservation to separate you from oncoming traffic, verges can be narrow or soft, and overtaking traffic may be travelling close to the centre line. Get the vehicle as far onto the verge as the ground allows, make yourself visible, and stand well back from the road edge while you wait.</p>
          </details>
